      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That critique touches on the legitimate concern of \"killer acquisitions,\" where large companies buy nascent competitors specifically to neutralize a future threat. Many economists and critics argue this practice prevents genuine market competition and entrenches monopolies, rather than solely driving free-market innovation. While some defend these acquisitions as strategic R&D, many view them as a mechanism for eliminating disruptive potential.",
      "source": "target",
